WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.217 --> 00:00:22.048
Hello I'm Faye Moss, and welcome to Superstars in History, the only talk show that brings you exclusive

2
00:00:22.128 --> 00:00:25.910
interviews with fabulous famous people from the past.

3
00:00:26.751 --> 00:00:30.253
I know you'll be as thrilled about today's guest as I am.

4
00:00:30.854 --> 00:00:36.639
He was a pilot and a spy during World War II, but you probably know him for his

5
00:00:36.879 --> 00:00:40.164
wacky and enormously entertaining children's books.

6
00:00:40.545 --> 00:00:47.675
Boys and girls, meet the author of James and the Giant Peach, Charlie and the Chocolate Factory, Matilda,

7
00:00:47.915 --> 00:00:50.859
and more Roald Dahl!

8
00:01:07.436 --> 00:01:09.538
Hello! Hi, Roald.

9
00:01:09.878 --> 00:01:13.861
Thanks so much for being here today on Superstars in History.

10
00:01:14.562 --> 00:01:18.465
Your books are so famous, they're like required reading for kids.

11
00:01:19.065 --> 00:01:23.229
Maybe that's because they feature kid characters in challenging situations.

12
00:01:23.889 --> 00:01:27.152
How much of your writing was inspired by your own childhood?

13
00:01:28.113 --> 00:01:31.275
That's quite a difficult question to answer, Faye.

14
00:01:32.196 --> 00:01:36.880
I was born in Wales in 1916, the third of five children.

15
00:01:37.676 --> 00:01:44.860
When I was three years old, my oldest sister suddenly died, and weeks later, my father died too.

16
00:01:45.761 --> 00:01:52.885
My mother considered moving the family to Norway, where my parents had been born, but she ultimately decided

17
00:01:52.945 --> 00:01:55.907
to stay in Wales and move later to England.

18
00:01:56.568 --> 00:02:02.151
You see, my father believed English schools were the best in the world and he wanted his children

19
00:02:02.231 --> 00:02:03.512
to be educated there.

20
00:02:04.756 --> 00:02:09.820
In your novel Matilda, you spend a lot of time focusing on Matilda's school life.

21
00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:16.344
There's even a terrifying headmistress famous for her violent and creative punishments.

22
00:02:16.804 --> 00:02:19.526
Did you draw on real-life experiences for this?

23
00:02:20.227 --> 00:02:23.909
Well, I do remember my school days quite vividly.

24
00:02:24.529 --> 00:02:28.732
Back then, punishments were very harsh, and they often hurt.

25
00:02:29.253 --> 00:02:32.455
My last boarding school was particularly violent.

26
00:02:32.895 --> 00:02:38.684
The teachers were brutal, and older students were allowed to beat and bully younger ones.

27
00:02:39.845 --> 00:02:40.586
Oh my.

28
00:02:40.766 --> 00:02:42.668
I can't even imagine.

29
00:02:43.148 --> 00:02:46.271
You must have terrible memories from your school days!

30
00:02:47.432 --> 00:02:48.694
It wasn't all bad.

31
00:02:49.214 --> 00:02:55.760
The great chocolate manufacturer, Cadbury, sent samples of chocolate to students at one of my schools.

32
00:02:56.221 --> 00:02:59.504
Then we'd fill out a form to let them know how we liked them.

33
00:03:00.424 --> 00:03:05.207
I used to dream of making a marvelous chocolate bar that would impress Mr.

34
00:03:05.247 --> 00:03:06.528
Cadbury himself.

35
00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:16.035
Hmm that sounds like something that may have inspired one of your books!

36
00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:20.258
Did you know early on that you wanted to be a writer?

37
00:03:21.178 --> 00:03:25.441
Not at all—in fact my teachers thought I was a terrible writer.

38
00:03:26.162 --> 00:03:33.794
After high school, I didn't attend university, and in 1934, I started working for an oil company.

39
00:03:34.294 --> 00:03:40.197
A few years later, they dispatched me to Africa, and I was there when World War II began.

40
00:03:40.797 --> 00:03:46.960
I joined the Royal Air Force and became a pilot, but before I even saw combat, I crashed

41
00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:48.560
my plane in the desert.

42
00:03:51.621 --> 00:03:53.082
Luckily you survived!

43
00:03:54.203 --> 00:03:59.630
Indeed! But my injuries were serious, and I spent a long time in a hospital.

44
00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:06.135
It took six months before I was declared fit to fly again, and my career as a pilot

45
00:04:06.235 --> 00:04:07.416
ended up quite brief.

46
00:04:08.017 --> 00:04:13.902
The injuries from my crash still affected me, so the RAF sent me back to England.

47
00:04:14.623 --> 00:04:19.867
You were only 24, and already you'd had such an exciting life!

48
00:04:20.387 --> 00:04:22.429
You weren't even a famous author yet.

49
00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:25.572
We're getting to that part.

50
00:04:26.308 --> 00:04:30.930
In 1942 I was sent to work in Washington D.C.

51
00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:32.331
at the British Embassy.

52
00:04:32.751 --> 00:04:37.673
Another British government worker was writing an article about the war for a magazine.

53
00:04:38.293 --> 00:04:44.676
He asked me for some recollections of my war experience, so I wrote down an account of my

54
00:04:44.756 --> 00:04:45.896
plane crash for him.

55
00:04:46.336 --> 00:04:50.778
Once he read my story, he decided to submit it, without making any changes!

56
00:04:51.419 --> 00:04:54.480
This inspired me to write more stories.

57
00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:00.043
Many of your early stories were about your short career as a pilot, right?

58
00:05:00.803 --> 00:05:05.806
Right. Walt Disney even wanted to make a movie out of one of my stories.

59
00:05:06.226 --> 00:05:12.389
The movie never got made, but it was adapted into a children's book that became popular with Eleanor

60
00:05:12.449 --> 00:05:15.471
Roosevelt. Walt Disney!

61
00:05:15.611 --> 00:05:16.872
Eleanor Roosevelt!

62
00:05:17.432 --> 00:05:19.993
Wow, you knew a lot of famous people.

63
00:05:20.634 --> 00:05:24.716
I've heard rumors that you even spied on President Roosevelt!

64
00:05:25.548 --> 00:05:31.650
I guess you could say I was a spy, but in truth, I was a very boring spy.

65
00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:39.652
The prime minister of the United Kingdom, Winston Churchill, wanted to stay informed about President Roosevelt's thoughts and

66
00:05:39.732 --> 00:05:44.393
feelings. My job was to pass on any such information.

67
00:05:44.873 --> 00:05:46.894
Not very exciting, you see!

68
00:05:47.774 --> 00:05:54.796
After the war, I married Patricia Neal, a Hollywood actress, started a family, and continued to write.

69
00:05:56.008 --> 00:06:00.612
It wasn't until 1960 that you started writing specifically for children.

70
00:06:01.193 --> 00:06:02.474
Why the sudden change?

71
00:06:03.535 --> 00:06:10.381
By 1960 I had run out of ideas for stories, and my publisher was becoming impatient.

72
00:06:10.941 --> 00:06:14.404
Meanwhile I'd been making up stories for my own children.

73
00:06:14.864 --> 00:06:20.169
I wrote one of them down and sent it to my publisher, and eventually it became James and

74
00:06:20.229 --> 00:06:21.470
the Giant Peach.

75
00:06:22.211 --> 00:06:27.107
By the end of the year, I had finished the first draft of Charlie and the Chocolate Factory.

76
00:06:28.468 --> 00:06:36.593
You achieved professional success in the 1960s, but they were a very hard time for you personally, weren't

77
00:06:36.653 --> 00:06:43.358
they? Yes, the 1960s were a time of great personal difficulty.

78
00:06:43.879 --> 00:06:47.781
My infant son was in a serious accident in 1960.

79
00:06:48.182 --> 00:06:51.084
He survived but was plagued with health issues.

80
00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:55.922
In 1962 my oldest daughter died of measles.

81
00:06:56.542 --> 00:07:02.784
And in 1965 my wife suffered a series of strokes, and she had to learn to walk and

82
00:07:02.844 --> 00:07:04.525
talk all over again.

83
00:07:04.545 --> 00:07:07.766
That sounds really challenging.

84
00:07:09.047 --> 00:07:15.209
It was, and through all of it, I continued writing and working on film and TV projects.

85
00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:21.512
Did you know that I wrote the original script for the movie Willy Wonka and the Chocolate Factory?

86
00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:23.500
Yes, I did.

87
00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:31.823
In total, you wrote 34 books that have been translated into 58 languages and have sold over 250

88
00:07:32.443 --> 00:07:33.744
million copies.

89
00:07:34.284 --> 00:07:36.285
That's quite a remarkable career!

90
00:07:36.985 --> 00:07:38.925
Thank you for joining me today, Roald.

91
00:07:39.266 --> 00:07:41.206
We'll let you get back to history now.

92
00:07:42.387 --> 00:07:48.624
Good-bye, Faye.

93
00:07:48.644 --> 00:07:52.627
Thanks, everyone, for tuning in for my interview with Roald Dahl.

94
00:07:53.348 --> 00:08:01.575
He continued his fantastic career until November 23, 1990, when he died at the age of 74.

95
00:08:02.075 --> 00:08:07.660
He left behind a large body of work that still brings joy and a love of reading to

96
00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:09.822
people all over the world.

97
00:08:10.683 --> 00:08:16.007
Our time is up for today, but I'll be back again soon with a new special guest from

98
00:08:16.067 --> 00:08:16.708
the past.

99
00:08:17.376 --> 00:08:19.198
You've been watching Faye Moss.

100
00:08:19.698 --> 00:08:20.359
Good-bye!
